  "outcome": {
    "ok": true,
    "turn_id": "d09_unique_value:r9:t8",
    "narration": "Iris sat on the edge of one of the two beds, the simple stool beside it untouched. She had been quiet through the last several exchanges, and she remained so now, her gaze moving across the sparse floor — the empty wardrobes, the bare medical cabinets against the far wall, the open central space where the spiral stair touched down and continued upward.\n\nAfter a moment she brushed a thumb across her own knuckles, a small, self-conscious gesture. \"I don't know yet,\" she said. \"I don't know what I need.\" Her voice carried no particular softness or hardness — just the plainness of someone stating what was true for her. She looked toward the windowless stone walls as though expecting them to offer something, then back. \"This is all very new. I haven't been here long enough to know what comfortable looks like.\"\n\nShe folded her hands in her lap, stilling the fidgeting. The room held its quiet. Nothing moved in the stairwell below, and the empty cabinets stood as they had — closed, bare, waiting for contents that had not arrived.",
